id: rust.tracing
kind: rust
title: tracing
aliases: tracing, structured logs, spans
tags: observability, async
questions:
  - how do you instrument an async service?
invariant: Spans carry context across awaits and tasks; events carry facts with structured fields.
answer_30s: Use a span per request and per outbound call, emit events with structured fields instead of formatted strings, and correlate with a propagated request id.
answer_2m: |
  The async-specific value is that span context travels with the task, so a log
  emitted deep inside a spawned task still knows which request it belongs to --
  string formatting cannot do that. Discipline that makes it useful: one span
  per inbound request and outbound dependency (method, route, status, latency),
  structured fields rather than interpolation, ids from headers, and ERROR
  reserved for things a human must act on. Log volume is a cost: high-cardinality
  values turn logging into an expensive database, so put measurements in metrics
  and facts in logs.
when_to_use:
  - every service; the seam between code and operations
minimal_example: |
  use tracing::{info, instrument};

  #[instrument(skip(state), fields(order_id = %id))]
  async fn create_order(state: AppState, id: String) -> Result<Order, AppError> {
      info!(items = 3, "creating order");
      Ok(load_order(&state, &id).await?)
  }
failure_modes:
  - formatted strings as messages, making logs unqueryable
  - logging secrets or whole payloads
  - debug-level instrumentation everywhere, burying the signal
related:
  - design.observability
  - rust.axum
  - k8s.logs
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tracing/latest/tracing/
